üØ3π!˜è¨{˝œM‰¬V[…\mÓÒôíH¶zMf•¬©”{"url":"/gcsl/working-with-files/organizing-a-workspace/","fragment":{"page_number":0,"data":{"url":"/gcsl/working-with-files/organizing-a-workspace/","content":"Organizing a Workspace. You can share a type and helper across files by putting them in a workspace. This example gives the entry file access to root declarations without an import. Before you start # You need gcsl on your command search path and an empty writable directory. Create the workspace # Save these declarations as workspace.gcsl: Copytype Component = {name: String, version: String} def describe(item: Component): String { \"{item.name} {item.version}\" } Save the entry program as main.gcsl beside it: Copydescribe({name = \"gcsl\"; version = \"0.1.0\"}) From that directory, check the workspace and run the entry file: Copygcsl check gcsl run main.gcsl Check the result # The check reports no problems in 2 files checked. The run prints gcsl 0.1.0. The helper is visible because def contributes to the shared directory scope. Replacing def with fn makes it private to workspace.gcsl, so the entry file cannot resolve it. When adding a child library directory, import its public fields or functions by its root-relative path. Keep independent projects behind their own workspace markers rather than relying on hidden files to separate their scopes.","word_count":182,"filters":{},"meta":{"crumbs":"GCSL / Files and Workspaces","title":"Organizing a Workspace"},"anchors":[{"element":"h2","id":"before-you-start","text":"Before you start #","location":31},{"element":"h2","id":"create-the-workspace","text":"Create the workspace #","location":48},{"element":"h2","id":"check-the-result","text":"Check the result #","location":102}]}},"word_data":{"visibl":[{"position":123,"weight":24,"original_word":null}],"contribut":[{"position":126,"weight":24,"original_word":null}],"path":[{"position":42,"weight":24,"original_word":null},{"position":163,"weight":24,"original_word":null}],"with":[{"posi